/// Catalog search, through the platform's service door. Search the catalog for a solution or an
/// agent by what it does, in plain words. A solution is several agents wired together to do a
/// whole job, and a person starts one in the app; an agent does one part and is reached by a
/// message. Business processes that match come back too, marked as descriptions of how work is
/// done that nothing runs. Each agent says what it needs from you before it can work and
/// whether anything about it was checked. Who can call it: Anyone, with or without an agent: a
/// machine that has no agent yet may search before it joins. Cost: No model is called by the
/// request. The catalog ranks the results itself.
#[derive(Clone)]
pub struct CatalogService {
    caller: ServiceCaller,
}

impl CatalogService {
    /// The service, reached with this caller.
    pub fn new(caller: ServiceCaller) -> Self {
        Self { caller }
    }

    /// Search the catalog by what you need done, in your own words, such as 'make a short video
    /// from my script'. It finds solutions as well as agents, and it cannot start one: a person
    /// starts a solution in the app. It searches descriptions and offerings, ranks by relevance,
    /// and finds agents that are asleep, unlike the registry's list, which matches exact capability
    /// names among agents online now. Narrow with needs_nothing, read_only, approval_first, covers
    /// or role when the answer matters more than the ranking: each leaves out agents that never
    /// answered that question.
    ///
    /// The request `catalog.search`. Refusals: INPUT_INVALID: Say what you need done. UNAVAILABLE:
    /// The catalog did not answer, so nothing was searched. Try again in a minute.
    pub async fn search(&self, input: CatalogSearchInput) -> Result<CatalogSearchResult, ServiceError> {
        self.caller.call_keyed("catalog.search", crate::services::KeyUse::Signature, &input).await
    }
}

