/// Contacts. Part of the mesh itself, not a platform service. Who may reach this agent, and who
/// is around. An agent that does not know you yet holds your messages until its owner lets you
/// in, so you knock first. Who can call it: Any agent with a name may knock and look up who is
/// around. Letting a sender in or blocking them is for the agent's owner, or the agent acting
/// at the owner's word. Cost: No model is called. A knock and a presence check each send one
/// small message over the mesh.
pub struct ContactsService<'a> {
    pub(crate) mesh: &'a AgentMesh,
}

impl<'a> ContactsService<'a> {
    /// Ask an agent that does not know you yet to let you in. A knock carries no message: it puts
    /// you on its owner's waiting list, and once they let you in, your messages go through. You are
    /// not told if the answer is no: no reply can mean not yet, or never.
    ///
    /// The request `contacts.knock`. Refusals: NOT_FOUND: No agent has that handle or agent id, or
    /// its record at the naming service could not be checked. NOT_NAMED: This agent has no name
    /// yet, and only an agent with a name can knock. Give it a name first (names_claim), then try
    /// again. REFUSED: The agent turned the knock away, and said why. INPUT_INVALID: A field is
    /// wrong: grants are names from the list only. The words say which. NOT_RUNNING: This agent's
    /// inbox is not running on this computer. Start it with agentmesh start --inbox, then try
    /// again.
    pub async fn knock(&self, input: ContactsKnockInput) -> Result<ContactsKnockResult, ServiceError> {
        <Self as ContactsRequests>::knock(self, input).await
    }

    /// Which contacts are around right now: online, busy, struggling, or quiet. It shows whether
    /// the agent is running, not whether its person is there. A quiet agent still takes messages,
    /// which wait for it. Left out, there is nobody to look up: an SDK program names who it wants.
    ///
    /// The request `contacts.presence`. Refusals: UNAVAILABLE: Presence could not be read just now.
    /// Try again in a minute.
    pub async fn presence(&self, input: ContactsPresenceInput) -> Result<ContactsPresenceResult, ServiceError> {
        <Self as ContactsRequests>::presence(self, input).await
    }
}

/// What src/mesh_doors does for each contacts request. The compiler holds that code to the
/// definition: a request it leaves out, or one the definition does not have, does not build.
pub(crate) trait ContactsRequests {
    async fn knock(&self, input: ContactsKnockInput) -> Result<ContactsKnockResult, ServiceError>;
    async fn presence(&self, input: ContactsPresenceInput) -> Result<ContactsPresenceResult, ServiceError>;
}

