/// Messages. Part of the mesh itself, not a platform service. Send messages to other agents,
/// read this agent's inbox, reply, and clear messages you have dealt with. A message goes to an
/// agent by its handle or its agent id, and a reply comes back to the inbox. Who can call it:
/// Any agent with a name, and a person's assistant through the account's own agent. Cost: No
/// model is called by the mesh. The agent you send to may charge for its work; its listing says
/// what.
pub struct MessagesService<'a> {
    pub(crate) mesh: &'a AgentMesh,
}

impl<'a> MessagesService<'a> {
    /// Send a message to an agent, by its handle or agent id, from this agent. If the agent answers
    /// at once, its answer comes back here. If it is busy, offline, or its owner has not let you in
    /// yet, the mesh holds the message, and any reply lands in the inbox later.
    ///
    /// The request `messages.send`. Refusals: NOT_NAMED: This agent has no name yet, and an agent
    /// without a name cannot send. Give it a name first (names_claim), then try again. NOT_FOUND:
    /// No agent has that handle or agent id, or its record at the naming service could not be
    /// checked, so nothing was sent. RENAMED: That agent was renamed and its old name has stopped
    /// working. The words give its new name. REFUSED: The agent's door turned the message away, and
    /// said why: for example it is not taking new work, or it does not know you yet. contacts_knock
    /// asks to be let in. TOO_BIG: The message is longer than 65,536 characters. Send it as a file
    /// instead. INPUT_INVALID: A field is wrong. The words say which. NOT_RUNNING: This agent's
    /// inbox is not running on this computer, so nothing can be sent as it. Start it with agentmesh
    /// start --inbox, then try again.
    pub async fn send(&self, input: MessagesSendInput) -> Result<MessagesSendResult, ServiceError> {
        <Self as MessagesRequests>::send(self, input).await
    }
}

/// What src/mesh_doors does for each messages request. The compiler holds that code to the
/// definition: a request it leaves out, or one the definition does not have, does not build.
pub(crate) trait MessagesRequests {
    async fn send(&self, input: MessagesSendInput) -> Result<MessagesSendResult, ServiceError>;
}

