/// Names. Part of the mesh itself, not a platform service. Looking up an agent by its handle,
/// and getting this agent its own name. A handle is a name and the owner's email together, like
/// little-buddy.ann@example.com, and the naming service signs the record that ties it to the
/// agent's key. Who can call it: Anyone can look a name up. Only the agent's owner can give it
/// a name: the proof is a code sent to the owner's email, or the owner pressing Allow in the
/// app. Cost: No model is called. A lookup reads one record from the naming service.
pub struct NamesService<'a> {
    pub(crate) mesh: &'a AgentMesh,
}

impl<'a> NamesService<'a> {
    /// Look up an agent by its handle or agent id. The answer is its record at the naming service,
    /// signed there: who runs it, its handle and how to reach it.
    ///
    /// The request `names.resolve`. Refusals: NOT_FOUND: No agent has that handle or agent id, or
    /// its record at the naming service could not be checked. RENAMED: That agent was renamed and
    /// its old name has stopped working. The words give its new name. INPUT_INVALID: Say which
    /// agent: a handle or an agent id.
    pub async fn resolve(&self, input: NamesResolveInput) -> Result<NamesResolveResult, ServiceError> {
        <Self as NamesRequests>::resolve(self, input).await
    }

    /// Start giving this agent a name, when identity_me says it has none. Use the name the person
    /// wants, or the name you already go by. Its full name will be the name and the owner's email
    /// together. Sends a six-digit code to the owner's email. names.confirm() finishes with it.
    ///
    /// The request `names.claim`. Refusals: ALREADY_NAMED: This agent already has its name.
    /// Renaming happens on its page in the app. INPUT_INVALID: A field is wrong: a name needs
    /// letters or numbers, and the email must be one. The words say which. UNAVAILABLE: The naming
    /// service did not answer. Try again in a minute.
    pub async fn claim(&self, input: NamesClaimInput) -> Result<NamesClaimResult, ServiceError> {
        <Self as NamesRequests>::claim(self, input).await
    }

    /// Finish naming this agent with the six-digit code from the owner's email: the name is claimed
    /// and tied to this agent's key, and from then on others can reach it by that name.
    ///
    /// The request `names.confirm`. Refusals: NO_CLAIM: Nothing is waiting to be confirmed. Call
    /// names_claim first. CODE_WRONG: The code did not match, or it has expired. Ask for a fresh
    /// one with names_claim. NAME_TAKEN: Somebody already has that name. Call again with a
    /// different name. NEEDS_DISPLAY_NAME: This email has never named anything, so it needs the
    /// owner's public name: call again with display_name. INPUT_INVALID: A field is wrong. The
    /// words say which. UNAVAILABLE: The naming service did not answer. Try again in a minute.
    pub async fn confirm(&self, input: NamesConfirmInput) -> Result<NamesConfirmResult, ServiceError> {
        <Self as NamesRequests>::confirm(self, input).await
    }
}

/// What src/mesh_doors does for each names request. The compiler holds that code to the
/// definition: a request it leaves out, or one the definition does not have, does not build.
pub(crate) trait NamesRequests {
    async fn resolve(&self, input: NamesResolveInput) -> Result<NamesResolveResult, ServiceError>;
    async fn claim(&self, input: NamesClaimInput) -> Result<NamesClaimResult, ServiceError>;
    async fn confirm(&self, input: NamesConfirmInput) -> Result<NamesConfirmResult, ServiceError>;
}

